WHY ENCRYPTION EXISTS
│
├── Purpose of Encryption
│   ├── Protection Goal
│   │   └── Protects the readability and meaning of data when someone
│   │       obtains it without the required cryptographic authority.
│   ├── What It Does Not Guarantee
│   │   └── Data may still be copied or stolen; encryption aims to keep
│   │       the obtained data unreadable.
│   └── Defense in Depth
│       └── Encryption works alongside authorization, secure application
│           handling, logging, and monitoring rather than replacing them.
│
├── Encryption Terminology
│   ├── Plaintext
│   │   └── Original readable data.
│   ├── Ciphertext
│   │   └── Data transformed into an encrypted, unreadable form.
│   ├── Cryptographic Key
│   │   └── Cryptographic material used as part of encryption or
│   │       decryption operations.
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   └── Converts plaintext into ciphertext.
│   └── Decryption
│       └── Converts ciphertext back into plaintext through an authorized
│           decryption path.
│
├── Basic Data Transformation
│   ├── Encryption Flow
│   │   └── Plaintext → Encryption → Ciphertext
│   └── Decryption Flow
│       └── Ciphertext → Authorized decryption → Plaintext
│
├── Authorization and Encryption
│   ├── Authorization
│   │   ├── Question Answered
│   │   │   └── Can this identity perform this action on this resource?
│   │   └── Example
│   │       └── Whether a Lambda execution role may retrieve an S3 object.
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   ├── Question Answered
│   │   │   └── Can these bytes be understood without the required key
│   │   │       access?
│   │   └── Protection Boundary
│   │       └── Protects data readability rather than deciding every
│   │           permitted resource operation.
│   └── Critical Distinction
│       ├── Authorization protects access to the resource.
│       ├── Encryption protects the meaning and readability of the data.
│       └── Secure systems commonly require both controls.
│
├── Resource Access and Key Usage
│   ├── Resource Permission
│   │   └── Determines whether the principal may access the encrypted
│   │       resource, such as retrieving an S3 object.
│   ├── Key Permission
│   │   └── Determines whether the principal may use the applicable key
│   │       through a permitted cryptographic operation.
│   └── Key Rules
│       ├── Resource access is not the same as key usage.
│       ├── Key usage is not the same as resource access.
│       ├── Access to an encrypted resource does not always guarantee
│       │   access to usable plaintext.
│       └── Permission related to a key does not automatically grant
│           access to every resource protected by that key.
│
├── Data States
│   ├── Data at Rest
│   │   ├── Definition
│   │   │   └── Data stored somewhere.
│   │   └── Examples
│   │       └── S3 objects, database records, EBS volumes, backups, and
│   │           snapshots.
│   ├── Data in Transit
│   │   ├── Definition
│   │   │   └── Data moving between systems.
│   │   └── Examples
│   │       └── Browser to API Gateway, Lambda to another API, or an
│   │           application to a database.
│   └── Data in Use
│       ├── Definition
│       │   └── Data currently being processed.
│       └── Examples
│           └── Decrypted information in application memory or a secret
│               temporarily loaded by an application.
│
├── Plaintext Exposure Boundary
│   ├── Authorized Use
│   │   └── An authorized application may decrypt protected information
│   │       because it needs plaintext for processing.
│   ├── Security Question
│   │   └── Where is plaintext allowed to exist, and who is allowed to
│   │       cause decryption?
│   ├── Application Responsibility
│   │   └── After decryption, the application must protect plaintext in
│   │       memory, logs, errors, caches, and exports.
│   └── Key Limitation
│       └── Encryption does not protect a new plaintext copy merely
│           because the original source was encrypted.
│
├── Validated S3 and Lambda Scenario
│   ├── Initial State
│   │   └── Customer records are stored encrypted in S3.
│   ├── Authorized Processing
│   │   └── Lambda is authorized to retrieve and decrypt the records.
│   ├── Exposure
│   │   └── Lambda writes the decrypted customer record into CloudWatch
│   │       Logs as plaintext.
│   ├── Failed Control
│   │   └── Application handling failed.
│   └── Controls That Did Not Fail
│       ├── S3 storage encryption worked correctly.
│       └── Key authorization worked as intended because Lambda was
│           authorized to decrypt the data.
│
├── Authentication and Business Authorization
│   ├── Authentication
│   │   └── Establishes who is making the request.
│   ├── Authorization
│   │   └── Establishes what actions the identity may perform.
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   └── Controls whether protected bytes can become readable.
│   └── Important Rule
│       └── Successful decryption does not prove that the application user
│           owns the record or that the business operation is authorized.
│
├── Corrected Decryption Model
│   ├── Unsafe Assumption
│   │   └── Do not always assume that the application directly receives a
│   │       decryption key and decrypts the data itself.
│   ├── Reason
│   │   └── The exact process depends on whether encryption is server-side
│   │       or client-side.
│   └── Valid Current Mental Model
│       └── Encrypted resource access + authorized decryption path →
│           plaintext becomes available → application must handle it
│           securely.
│
├── Common Misconceptions
│   ├── Encryption prevents data theft
│   │   └── Data can still be copied; encryption protects its readability.
│   ├── Encryption removes the need for authorization
│   │   └── Authorization and encryption protect different boundaries.
│   ├── IAM denial makes encryption unnecessary
│   │   └── Encryption adds protection when data is exposed through
│   │       another path.
│   ├── Encrypted data never becomes plaintext
│   │   └── Applications normally decrypt data before processing it.
│   ├── Storage encryption protects plaintext logs
│   │   └── Plaintext copied to logs requires separate protection.
│   └── Decryption proves correct business access
│       └── Decryption authority does not validate record ownership or
│           application-level authorization.
│
└── Exam Reasoning
    ├── Identify the sensitive data.
    ├── Determine whether it is at rest, in transit, or in use.
    ├── Identify who can access the resource.
    ├── Identify who can use the key or decryption path.
    ├── Locate where ciphertext becomes plaintext.
    └── Examine how plaintext is handled after decryption.


ENCRYPTION FUNDAMENTALS AND CRYPTOGRAPHIC OPERATIONS
│
├── Cryptography
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── The broader practice of protecting information through
│   │       mathematical techniques.
│   └── Security Guarantees
│       ├── Confidentiality
│       ├── Integrity
│       ├── Authenticity
│       └── Evidence supporting non-repudiation
│
├── Confidentiality
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Prevents unauthorized people or systems from reading
│   │       information.
│   ├── Primary Mechanism
│   │   └── Encryption transforms readable plaintext into ciphertext.
│   └── Limitation
│       └── Does not necessarily hide the existence, location, size, or
│           surrounding metadata of the protected data.
│
├── Integrity
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Detects whether data was modified after creation or
│   │       transmission.
│   ├── Example
│   │   └── Detecting a transfer amount changed from 1,000 to 9,000.
│   └── Distinction from Confidentiality
│       ├── Confidentiality asks whether unauthorized parties can read
│       │   the data.
│       └── Integrity asks whether the data was changed.
│
├── Authenticity
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Establishes whether data came from the expected source or
│   │       someone possessing the expected cryptographic authority.
│   └── Example
│       └── Verifying that a software package came from the intended
│           company.
│
├── Non-Repudiation
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Evidence that makes it difficult for a sender to credibly
│   │       deny a signed cryptographic action.
│   ├── Supporting Mechanism
│   │   └── Digital signatures can provide evidence supporting
│   │       non-repudiation.
│   └── Dependencies
│       ├── Secure control of the private key
│       ├── Trustworthy identity-to-key connection
│       ├── Absence of key compromise
│       └── Reliable surrounding audit evidence
│
├── Encryption
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Converts readable plaintext into unreadable ciphertext using
│   │       a cryptographic algorithm and key.
│   ├── Flow
│   │   └── Plaintext + key → Encryption algorithm → Ciphertext
│   └── Primary Guarantee
│       └── Confidentiality
│
├── Decryption
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Converts compatible ciphertext back into plaintext.
│   ├── Flow
│   │   └── Ciphertext + required key operation → Plaintext
│   └── Key Relationship
│       ├── A compatible key operation can recover the plaintext.
│       └── An unrelated key should fail to recover the plaintext.
│
├── Hashing
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Transforms input data into a fixed-size output called a hash
│   │       or digest.
│   ├── Flow
│   │   └── Input data → Hash function → Fixed-size digest
│   ├── Properties
│   │   ├── The same input produces the same digest.
│   │   ├── A small input change should produce a very different digest.
│   │   ├── The original input cannot practically be recovered from the
│   │   │   digest.
│   │   └── Finding different inputs with the same digest should be
│   │       extremely difficult.
│   ├── One-Way Nature
│   │   └── There is no normal Decrypt(hash) operation.
│   ├── Collision
│   │   ├── Definition
│   │   │   └── Two different inputs producing the same digest.
│   │   └── Security Requirement
│   │       └── A secure hash makes deliberately finding a collision
│   │           extremely difficult.
│   └── Correction
│       └── Hashing is deterministic and one-way but is not mathematically
│           one-to-one.
│
├── Encryption vs Hashing
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   └── Designed to be reversed through an authorized operation.
│   └── Hashing
│       └── Designed as a one-way transformation.
│
├── Plain Digest Verification
│   ├── What a Match Proves
│   │   └── The downloaded file matches the supplied expected digest.
│   ├── What a Match Does Not Prove
│   │   └── The intended company published the file or digest.
│   ├── Attacker-Controlled File and Digest
│   │   ├── Attacker replaces the original file with a malicious file.
│   │   ├── Attacker calculates the malicious file's digest.
│   │   ├── Attacker replaces the original digest.
│   │   └── Customer's calculated digest matches while authenticity is
│   │       absent.
│   └── Trusted-Channel Requirement
│       ├── A plain digest is useful when the expected value arrives
│       │   through a trusted, unmodified channel.
│       └── A plain digest provides no meaningful authenticity when the
│           attacker controls both the file and expected digest.
│
├── Digital Signature
│   ├── Creation
│   │   ├── Data is hashed.
│   │   └── The digest is signed using the signer's private key.
│   ├── Guarantees
│   │   ├── Integrity
│   │   │   └── Modified data causes verification to fail.
│   │   ├── Authenticity
│   │   │   └── A valid signature was created using the corresponding
│   │   │       private key.
│   │   └── Evidence supporting non-repudiation
│   └── Limitation
│       └── Signing does not make data confidential or unreadable.
│
├── Signature Verification
│   ├── Inputs
│   │   ├── Received data
│   │   ├── Digital signature
│   │   └── Corresponding public key
│   ├── Result
│   │   └── Valid or invalid
│   ├── Meaning of Valid Result
│   │   ├── The signature corresponds to the received data.
│   │   ├── The corresponding private key created the signature.
│   │   └── The data was not modified after signing.
│   └── Identity Limitation
│       └── The verifier must separately trust that the public key belongs
│           to the claimed company.
│
├── Digital Signature vs Plain Hash
│   ├── Plain Hash
│   │   └── Asks whether data matches a particular digest.
│   ├── Digital Signature
│   │   └── Asks whether data matches what the holder of the corresponding
│   │       private key signed.
│   └── Attacker Limitation
│       └── An attacker can create a new file and digest but cannot create
│           a valid company signature without the company's private key.
│
├── Public-Key Trust
│   ├── Requirement
│   │   └── Customers need a trusted company public key or a trusted
│   │       certificate connecting the key to the company.
│   └── Untrusted-Key Attack
│       ├── Attacker generates a separate key pair.
│       ├── Attacker signs malicious data using the attacker's private key.
│       └── Attacker falsely labels the attacker's public key as the
│           company's key.
│
├── Symmetric Encryption
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Uses the same shared secret key for encryption and decryption.
│   ├── Advantages
│   │   ├── Fast
│   │   ├── Efficient for large amounts of data
│   │   └── Suitable for files, objects, database values, and network
│   │       traffic
│   └── Key-Distribution Problem
│       └── Every authorized encrypting or decrypting system needs secure
│           access to the shared secret.
│
├── Asymmetric Cryptography
│   ├── Key Pair
│   │   ├── Public key can be distributed.
│   │   └── Private key must remain protected.
│   ├── Confidentiality Flow
│   │   ├── Public key encrypts.
│   │   └── Corresponding private key decrypts.
│   ├── Signature Flow
│   │   ├── Private key signs.
│   │   └── Corresponding public key verifies.
│   └── Important Distinction
│       └── Signing is a distinct cryptographic operation and should not
│           be described merely as ordinary encryption using a private
│           key.
│
├── Symmetric and Asymmetric Relationship
│   ├── Symmetric Strength
│   │   └── Efficient encryption of large application data.
│   ├── Asymmetric Strength
│   │   └── Public distribution, trust establishment, and public
│   │       verification.
│   └── Combined Use
│       └── Asymmetric cryptography can help establish trust or protect
│           key material while symmetric encryption protects large data.
│
├── Cryptographic Keys
│   ├── Definition
│   │   └── Generated cryptographic material intended for use by an
│   │       algorithm.
│   ├── Distinction from Passwords
│   │   ├── Passwords are commonly selected or entered by humans.
│   │   └── Cryptographic keys are generated for algorithmic use.
│   └── Security Rule
│       └── Strong encryption can be defeated if the key is exposed.
│
├── Algorithm and Key Secrecy
│   ├── Algorithm
│   │   └── May be publicly known.
│   ├── Key
│   │   └── Must receive the required confidentiality and access
│   │       protection.
│   └── Design Rule
│       └── Security should not depend on hiding the algorithm.
│
├── Nonces and Initialization Values
│   ├── Nonce
│   │   └── A value intended for one-time use in its required
│   │       cryptographic context.
│   ├── Encryption Model
│   │   └── Plaintext + key + required unique value → Ciphertext
│   └── Effect
│       └── Identical plaintext encrypted under the same key can produce
│           different ciphertext.
│
├── Authenticated Encryption
│   ├── Guarantees
│   │   ├── Confidentiality
│   │   └── Integrity protection
│   ├── Output
│   │   └── Ciphertext plus an authentication tag.
│   └── Decryption Behavior
│       ├── Valid authentication tag → Decrypt
│       └── Invalid authentication tag → Reject
│
├── Encryption and Obfuscation
│   ├── Obfuscation
│   │   └── Makes information less obvious or harder to read casually.
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   └── Uses established algorithms and keys to provide defined
│   │       security guarantees.
│   └── Engineering Rule
│       └── Use trusted algorithms, libraries, and managed integrations
│           instead of inventing custom cryptography.
│
├── Validated Software-Package Scenario
│   ├── Requirements
│   │   ├── The package may remain publicly readable.
│   │   ├── Customers must verify the publisher.
│   │   └── Customers must detect modification.
│   ├── Required Guarantees
│   │   ├── Authenticity
│   │   └── Integrity
│   ├── Correct Operation
│   │   └── Digitally sign the package rather than primarily encrypting it.
│   ├── Company Operation
│   │   └── Sign using the company's private key.
│   ├── Customer Operation
│   │   └── Verify using the trusted company public key.
│   └── Plain-Hash Weakness
│       └── An attacker controlling the download location can replace both
│           the package and its unprotected digest.
│
└── Cryptographic Requirement Selection
    ├── Hide content
    │   └── Use encryption for confidentiality.
    ├── Detect modification
    │   └── Use integrity protection.
    ├── Verify source
    │   └── Use authenticity mechanisms such as digital signatures.
    ├── Create a one-way representation
    │   └── Use cryptographic hashing.
    ├── Efficiently encrypt large data
    │   └── Use symmetric encryption.
    └── Enable public verification
        └── Use asymmetric cryptography and a trusted public key.